# =============================================================================
# CITS5206 Capstone — Research Infrastructure Costing & Pricing Tool
#
# This repository is public-facing coursework. The client's material is not.
# The rule of thumb: if the client sent it, if it records a voice, or if it
# would let someone authenticate as us, it does not go in a commit.
#
# Ignoring a file does NOT remove it from history. If something confidential
# has already been committed, say so on the team channel and rewrite it out
# before pushing further.
#
# Check any single path with:   git check-ignore -v <path>
# Deliberately commit anything: git add -f <path>   (get agreement first)
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Client material
#
# Everything the client hands us — costing spreadsheets, pricing templates,
# worked examples. Relatively sensitive, and not ours to publish. Keep the
# originals in the team's Teams area; commit only our own summaries of them.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
reference/client/*
!reference/client/.gitkeep
!reference/client/README.md

# Unit-provided source material (UWA / third-party copyright).
# Our markdown summaries are committed; the originals are not.
reference/unit/*.pdf


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Office documents, by type
#
# Belt and braces for section 1 — a client spreadsheet dropped anywhere in the
# tree is still ignored. This also enforces the repository's format policy:
# artefacts are markdown or HTML so that every change diffs in GitHub.
# See presentations/STYLE-GUIDE.md §8.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.doc
*.docx
*.docm
*.dot
*.dotx
*.rtf
*.odt

*.xls
*.xlsx
*.xlsm
*.xlsb
*.xlt
*.xltx
*.ods

*.ppt
*.pptx
*.pptm
*.potx
*.odp

# Office lock / autosave leftovers
~$*
*.wbk
*.asd


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Recordings and transcripts
#
# Meeting audio and video capture identifiable voices, and transcripts capture
# unreviewed speech about identifiable people. Neither is committed. Write
# minutes from them in markdown, commit the minutes, and keep the source in the
# Teams area.
#
# Extension patterns alone do not catch a transcript pasted into a .md file, so
# the *-transcript.md rule below closes that gap. Name transcript files that way
# and they stay local by default.
#
# There is no internal-meeting exception. An earlier version of this file allowed
# a raw transcript to be committed when no client or other external party was
# present; that has been withdrawn. It is unreviewed speech either way, and the
# minutes are the artefact anyone actually needs.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.m4a
*.mp3
*.wav
*.aac
*.flac
*.ogg
*.opus
*.wma
*.amr

*.mp4
*.mov
*.mkv
*.avi
*.webm
*.wmv

*.srt
*.vtt
*.sbv
*.dfxp
*.ttml

# Transcripts written up as text, wherever they sit in the tree
*-transcript.md
*-transcript.txt


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3a. Our own internal review notes
#
# Audits of our own documentation: working critique, written for us, revised as
# we go. They are how we find our own mistakes, not a deliverable, and a
# half-finished list of everything wrong with the project is not something to
# hand a client or a facilitator. Findings that matter get actioned in the
# documents themselves, which is where they belong.
#
# These live in docs/internal/, whose README is committed so the folder and its
# rule are visible. The docs/audit-*.md line below is belt and braces: a review
# note written in the wrong place is still ignored.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/internal/*
!docs/internal/README.md

docs/audit-*.md


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3b. Scratch exports from the GitHub API
#
# scripts/ reads a `gh issue list --json ...` dump to work out what already
# exists before it changes anything. That dump is a snapshot of live state,
# stale the moment anyone touches an issue, and committing it invites someone
# to trust it. Re-export it; never read it out of a commit.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
issues.json
projects.json


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Credentials and secrets
#
# Nothing here is currently needed, but the calculation service will have a
# database and an auth configuration. The time to write these lines is before
# that exists, not after.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.sample
!.env.template

*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.cer
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.*
*.ppk

secrets.*
credentials.*
service-account*.json
.htpasswd
.netrc
.pgpass


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Local data
#
# Real costing figures must never end up in a fixture. Test data is synthetic
# and lives in a clearly named fixtures directory; local databases and dumps
# do not get committed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
# SQLite's write-ahead log and shared-memory files sit beside the database and
# carry the same data. They were previously caught only by an enumeration of
# database filenames in src/.gitignore, which went stale as the development
# store was versioned forward; matched by pattern here instead.
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.dump
*.bak
/data/
/uploads/
/local/
/private/
/tmp/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Dependencies, builds and caches
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node
node_modules/
.npm/
.pnpm-store/
.yarn/
*.tsbuildinfo
.eslintcache
.vite/
.next/
.nuxt/
.parcel-cache/
dist/
build/
out/
coverage/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# .NET
#
# These live here, at the root, and not only in src/.gitignore. That file covers
# what sits beneath it, so the first project added outside src/ — tests/ — brought
# its own bin/ and obj/ back with it, which is the same mistake ADR-001 action 5
# cleaned up for src/ in August. A rule that only guards one directory guards one
# directory.
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
TestResults/
*.user
*.suo
.vs/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/

# Containers and infrastructure
docker-compose.override.yml
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Operating system and editor noise
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride

Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db:encryptable
ehthumbs.db
ehthumbs_vista.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.lnk

.idea/

# VS Code: personal preferences stay out, shared project setup goes in.
#
# launch.json, tasks.json and settings.json are checked in deliberately: they are
# how a new team member gets the application running with F5 instead of by being
# told a sequence of commands, and they are what keeps everyone's formatting on
# the right side of the CI gate. Anything genuinely personal — window layout,
# private snippets — lands in the ignored remainder.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
!.vscode/settings.json
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.\#*
\#*\#
.project
.classpath
.settings/
